<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A youth group in Kisii makes <m>x</m> soapstone carvings and <m>y</m> soapstone bowls a week. Each of the soapstone carvings needs <m>5</m> hours of carving time and each of the soapstone bowls needs <m>6</m>; there are <m>47</m> hours of carving time. Each of the soapstone carvings needs <m>7</m> hours of polishing time and each of the soapstone bowls needs <m>4</m>; there are <m>59</m> hours of polishing time. The profit is Ksh <m>600</m> on each of the soapstone carvings and Ksh <m>500</m> on each of the soapstone bowls.</p><p>Give a whole-number plan that satisfies both conditions and makes a profit of at least Ksh <m>{4500}</m>. There is more than one right answer.</p><p><m>x =</m> <fillin characters="4" name="ans1"/>  <m>y =</m> <fillin characters="4" name="ans2"/> </p><div/></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>The conditions are <m>{5}x + {6}y \le {47}</m> and <m>{7}x + {4}y \le {59}</m>, with <m>x \ge 0</m>, <m>y \ge 0</m>. The profit is <m>P = {600}x + {500}y</m>. Plans near the corner where the two boundary lines meet give the most profit.</p><p>For example <m>x = {5}</m>, <m>y = {3}</m> needs <m>{43}</m> hours of carving time and <m>{47}</m> hours of polishing time, and makes Ksh <m>{4500}</m>. There are <m>{5}</m> plans that work.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>5</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>3</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>